WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:03.035
-Our next guest
is an Emmy-nominated actor

00:00:03.035 --> 00:00:04.971
you know from his work
on shows such as "Fleabag,"

00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:06.406
"Sherlock," and "Black Mirror"

00:00:06.406 --> 00:00:08.507
and films like "Spectre"
and "1917."

00:00:08.507 --> 00:00:10.542
He stars in
"All of Us Strangers,"

00:00:10.542 --> 00:00:11.945
which is in theaters now.

00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:14.547
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:16.349
-It's funny...

00:00:18.918 --> 00:00:21.187
Things are better now.
Of course they are. But...

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:26.458 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:26.458 --> 00:00:29.595
Doesn't take much to make you
feel the way you felt.

00:00:29.595 --> 00:00:32.298 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:32.298 --> 00:00:34.834
Back there again...

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:37.670 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.373
-Please welcome to the show
Andrew Scott, everybody!

00:00:40.373 --> 00:00:42.275
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:42.275 --> 00:00:56.989 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:56.989 --> 00:00:59.425
Welcome to the show.
I'm so happy to have you here.

00:00:59.425 --> 00:01:00.793
-Glad to be here. Thanking you.

00:01:00.793 --> 00:01:02.829
-I have been a fan
for a long time.

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:04.631
I think --
I had a weird thing happen,

00:01:04.631 --> 00:01:06.299
and I heard
that one of the reasons

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:10.136
you said yes to "Fleabag"
and playing the hot priest is...

00:01:10.136 --> 00:01:12.605
that you...
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:12.605 --> 00:01:15.275
-Easy, easy.
-Easy. Easy.

00:01:15.275 --> 00:01:18.878
-That you had actually been
sort of playing villains

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:21.181
and wanted to break free
of the villain roles.

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:22.615
-Yeah, so, when I was in my 20s,

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:24.350
I had a little baby --
little Irish, baby,

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:25.851
little puppy -- little face.

00:01:25.851 --> 00:01:28.554
And I really felt like I had
this kind of darkness inside me.

00:01:28.554 --> 00:01:31.791
I was like, "Why can't I get
get a part as a villain?"

00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:33.393
And then that happened.

00:01:33.393 --> 00:01:35.028
And then there was loads
of villains happening.

00:01:35.028 --> 00:01:36.529
So then I was like,
"Why can't I get a....

00:01:36.529 --> 00:01:38.998
Why can't I -- Why can't
people see the real me?!"

00:01:38.998 --> 00:01:41.734
Uh, and, uh --
So then Phoebe and I

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:45.905
had done a play together
in London that nobody saw.

00:01:45.905 --> 00:01:49.242
Uh, and then
she came a-knocking,

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:50.843
and that's where the priest
came from.

00:01:50.843 --> 00:01:52.945
-It does speak to what
a great actor you are

00:01:52.945 --> 00:01:54.980
because when I was
watching "Fleabag,"

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:57.516
I had that moment of, like,
"Where do I know him from?"

00:01:57.516 --> 00:01:59.085
-Right.
-And even though you look

00:01:59.085 --> 00:02:01.220
exactly the same
in "Sherlock" as Moriarty,

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:02.655
like, you were so --

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:04.624
You went from so villainous
to so charming that I thought,

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:06.259
"That can't possibly be
the same person."

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:07.860
-Well, we contain multitudes.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:09.662
-You do.
-We contain multitudes.

00:02:09.662 --> 00:02:11.530
-I don't. You do.
-Yeah. You're all dark.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:14.300
-Yeah. All dark.
I will say Phoebe, did you,

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.402
I mean, the finest compliment
she could ever do.

00:02:16.402 --> 00:02:19.138
The fact that she gave you
a character named Hot Priest.

00:02:19.138 --> 00:02:20.639
-Well, she didn't, you know.
-Really?

00:02:20.639 --> 00:02:21.908
-Other people did it?

00:02:21.908 --> 00:02:23.509
-Yeah, The character
was called Priest, you know,

00:02:23.509 --> 00:02:26.012
and then it was -- it was --

00:02:26.012 --> 00:02:27.847
it was these filthy people
that did it.

00:02:27.847 --> 00:02:29.281
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:29.281 --> 00:02:31.884
Yes!

00:02:31.884 --> 00:02:35.721
That's -- That's the power
of the Internet, you know, so...

00:02:35.721 --> 00:02:37.423
She mentions
that the character is hot,

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:39.391
and then the Internet
just, you know...

00:02:39.391 --> 00:02:41.093
-And I think --
You know, don't --

00:02:41.093 --> 00:02:42.495
You brought a lot to that, too.

00:02:42.495 --> 00:02:45.931
Your wonderful co-star
in this film, uh, Paul Mescal --

00:02:45.931 --> 00:02:47.567
he mentioned
that he was a fan.

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:50.636
He even dressed up
as Hot Priest.

00:02:50.636 --> 00:02:52.672
And that's, uh,
Daisy Edgar-Jones.

00:02:52.672 --> 00:02:55.041
-Yes. Yeah.
They dressed up for Halloween.

00:02:55.041 --> 00:02:57.543
They didn't just dress up
for no reason.

00:02:57.543 --> 00:02:59.745
-Very kind of you for --
They're not insane.

00:02:59.745 --> 00:03:01.747
It was a holiday.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:03:01.747 --> 00:03:03.216
-Um, this is such a --

00:03:03.216 --> 00:03:06.352
This movie, it's hard to talk
about, we were saying backstage.

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:08.288
-Yes. Very hard to talk about.
Yeah.

00:03:08.288 --> 00:03:10.189
-It is.
It's very sad, but it's more

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:11.857
beautiful than sad. Would you
say that's a fair way...

00:03:11.857 --> 00:03:13.525
-Yeah. I think that's exactly
right. It's very hopeful.

00:03:13.525 --> 00:03:15.161
It's -- The basic premise of it

00:03:15.161 --> 00:03:17.230
is that it's this man
who conjures up his parents

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:18.865
who've been killed
in a car accident,

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:21.133
and he talks to them as he
remembers them most acutely,

00:03:21.133 --> 00:03:23.235
which is when they are
slightly younger than he is.

00:03:23.235 --> 00:03:26.339
So he has these conversations
with his parents,

00:03:26.339 --> 00:03:27.873
who are played
by Claire Foy and Jamie Bell,

00:03:27.873 --> 00:03:29.375
both of whom are younger
than me.

00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:31.477
-Uh-huh.
-And at the same time,

00:03:31.477 --> 00:03:34.013
he's falling in love
with Paul Mescal.

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:35.481
-It is really something.

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:37.350
It's unlike anything
I've ever seen before.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.418
-Yeah.
-And I expected this

00:03:39.418 --> 00:03:41.186
beautiful romance,
and I got that.

00:03:41.186 --> 00:03:43.155
-Yeah.
-But also, as a parent,

00:03:43.155 --> 00:03:47.159
it was incredibly moving,
this idea that not just

00:03:47.159 --> 00:03:48.894
what your kids would say to you
all these years later,

00:03:48.894 --> 00:03:50.896
but what you would want
to be able to say to your kids,

00:03:50.896 --> 00:03:52.532
which, as a young parent,

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:54.667
you forget
that if you're not around,

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:56.970
you never have this moment
to say, "Hey, I was young, too."

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:59.038
-Yeah, well,
that's what's so beau--

00:03:59.038 --> 00:04:00.372
It's just this beautiful idea.

00:04:00.372 --> 00:04:02.408
And, you know, we're children
forever with our parents.

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:05.044
And, you know, everybody has a
relationship with their parents,

00:04:05.044 --> 00:04:06.746
even if they're not around.

00:04:06.746 --> 00:04:08.414
So it's this
really, really beautiful

00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:11.684
and very, very hopeful
kind of film.

00:04:11.684 --> 00:04:13.953
And, yeah, I think
that's what the film is --

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:15.487
is just taking off now.

00:04:15.487 --> 00:04:17.556
So it's so beautiful to hear
everybody's reaction to it.

00:04:17.556 --> 00:04:18.891
-There's a really wonderful
scene

00:04:18.891 --> 00:04:21.860
where you're sort of
visiting with your parents

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:23.229
and you're wearing --

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:25.030
As an adult,
you're wearing child's pajamas.

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:26.732
Now, they're sized for you.
-Yes.

00:04:26.732 --> 00:04:27.934
-And it's one of the most

00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:30.837
heartbreaking scenes
in the film.

00:04:30.837 --> 00:04:33.705
But I also couldn't help
but think when they said, "Cut,"

00:04:33.705 --> 00:04:37.376
you were just basically
in mortifying pajamas.

00:04:37.376 --> 00:04:39.579
-You know those polyester
pajamas that you have

00:04:39.579 --> 00:04:41.614
when you're a child
and you have such a --

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:45.484
I just looked ridiculous.
Talk about un-hot. Un-hot.

00:04:45.484 --> 00:04:47.487
There was no --
There was no Hot Priest there,

00:04:47.487 --> 00:04:49.889
but, uh, yeah, it was, um...

00:04:49.889 --> 00:04:52.691
Yeah, it seems absurd,
but it was -- it's very moving.

00:04:52.691 --> 00:04:55.361
-It's very moving.
I will say I've also seen

00:04:55.361 --> 00:04:56.929
a lot of press
with you and Paul.

00:04:56.929 --> 00:04:58.897
Uh, you seem like
very good friends.

00:04:58.897 --> 00:05:00.966
You seem to have a lot of laughs
with each other.

00:05:00.966 --> 00:05:03.202
And yet this film
is sort of almost --

00:05:03.202 --> 00:05:05.137
You know, it's so serious.
-It's -- Yeah.

00:05:05.137 --> 00:05:08.007
-Was it easy for you
to go both in and out of scenes

00:05:08.007 --> 00:05:09.775
and also just sort of
have a lightness

00:05:09.775 --> 00:05:11.744
that is obviously there
in your friendship?

00:05:11.744 --> 00:05:13.245
-Oh, totally.
When you're doing a sad --

00:05:13.245 --> 00:05:16.316
when you're doing anything sad,
you always have the most fun.

00:05:16.316 --> 00:05:17.950
You really do
because you can't ---

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:20.452
You can't spend your whole day
crying into the darkness.

00:05:20.452 --> 00:05:22.388
Do you know what I mean?
You really can't.

00:05:22.388 --> 00:05:24.189
And, uh, yeah,
I remember when I --

00:05:24.189 --> 00:05:26.292
when I played Hamlet,
we had such a laugh backstage,

00:05:26.292 --> 00:05:28.026
and then you're going on
and you're, like,

00:05:28.026 --> 00:05:29.996
talking about, you know,
really, you know --

00:05:29.996 --> 00:05:32.064
You're literally talking
about life and death.

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:34.967
But, yeah, you got to have
a bit of light in shade.

00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:37.203
-That's true. And, by the way,
on sets of comedies,

00:05:37.203 --> 00:05:39.538
like, that's where -- The minute
anybody says, "Cut,"

00:05:39.538 --> 00:05:41.407
everybody's like,
"Was that funny?"

00:05:41.407 --> 00:05:43.742
-Exactly. You have people
on headphones going,

00:05:43.742 --> 00:05:45.311
"That's funny. Yeah.
That's funny.

00:05:45.311 --> 00:05:47.546
Yeah, yeah. Mm-hmm.
Yeah, yeah. It's true.

00:05:47.546 --> 00:05:48.914
It's really true.
But such is life.

00:05:48.914 --> 00:05:50.182
-You asked about the response,

00:05:50.182 --> 00:05:51.784
and I know you've been out
doing a lot of press.

00:05:51.784 --> 00:05:53.653
I mean,
making a movie like this

00:05:53.653 --> 00:05:55.354
that is so unlike anything else,

00:05:55.354 --> 00:05:57.423
um, has it been --
has it been wonderful

00:05:57.423 --> 00:05:59.258
the way it's being received?
I have to imagine it is.

00:05:59.258 --> 00:06:01.227
-It's been phenomenal. It's...

00:06:01.227 --> 00:06:03.428
It's a film that's really,
really difficult to talk to,

00:06:03.428 --> 00:06:04.730
but to see the film, I believe,

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.198
is to love the film.
I really do.

00:06:06.198 --> 00:06:08.567
I think it affects people
in this extraordinary way.

00:06:08.567 --> 00:06:10.336
People are, like,
leaving the cinema.

00:06:10.336 --> 00:06:12.037
And it's not depressing,
as you say,

00:06:12.037 --> 00:06:14.273
but it's -- I'm determined
that people don't think

00:06:14.273 --> 00:06:16.008
it's a really depressing thing.

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:19.044
But it's so -- it's sort of
cathartic and moving.

00:06:19.044 --> 00:06:20.546
It's very emotional.
It's about families.

00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:21.614
And people just love that.

00:06:21.614 --> 00:06:22.915
People like a good cry
in the cinema.

00:06:22.915 --> 00:06:24.116
-I agree.
-You know.

00:06:24.116 --> 00:06:26.052
And there's a bit
of romance in there,

00:06:26.052 --> 00:06:27.353
and it's really,
really beautiful.

00:06:27.353 --> 00:06:29.889
So please just go and see it!
-I will say this.

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:30.990
Do you...

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:32.324
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:32.324 --> 00:06:34.293
Do you find
you're more emotional

00:06:34.293 --> 00:06:36.028
when you see a movie
on a plane?

00:06:36.028 --> 00:06:38.230
-Oh, my God. I had to be --

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:39.398
I had to be the --

00:06:39.398 --> 00:06:41.500
I remember the air steward
had to say,

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:43.268
"Are you alright, sir?"
Because I was watching --

00:06:43.268 --> 00:06:45.037
You know the first 20 minutes
of "Up"?

00:06:45.037 --> 00:06:46.172
Ever seen "Up"?
-Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:06:46.172 --> 00:06:48.173
-Oh, my God.
I fell on this...

00:06:48.173 --> 00:06:50.376
-They shouldn't allow that
on a plane. Yeah.

00:06:50.376 --> 00:06:52.978
-Oh, my God.
I was fully, like --

00:06:52.978 --> 00:06:54.413
[Sobbing] kind of crying.

00:06:54.413 --> 00:06:56.415
-Because I will say
there's often times where --

00:06:56.415 --> 00:06:58.317
You know, I have
a lot of guests on the show.

00:06:58.317 --> 00:07:00.118
Sometimes I have to, like,
catch the films or I can't.

00:07:00.118 --> 00:07:01.387
I can't always go see them
in the cinema.

00:07:01.387 --> 00:07:02.688
I don't watch them
the best way.

00:07:02.688 --> 00:07:04.757
Sometimes I'm watching them
on a laptop in an office.

00:07:04.757 --> 00:07:06.725
-Yeah.
And this movie was still

00:07:06.725 --> 00:07:08.460
getting me so much,
and I had a moment of like...

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:10.996
Oh, my God, if people
watch this on a plane,

00:07:10.996 --> 00:07:13.298
there should be
an announcement saying,

00:07:13.298 --> 00:07:15.034
"You have to let
the people in your row

00:07:15.034 --> 00:07:16.769
know you're about to do this.
-Exactly.

00:07:16.769 --> 00:07:18.437
-So they don't come to us
and be like,

00:07:18.437 --> 00:07:19.905
"Somebody's having
a mental breakdown."

00:07:19.905 --> 00:07:22.508
-Yeah.
"We're about to serve lunch.

00:07:22.508 --> 00:07:24.877
and "All of Us Strangers"
is about to start.

00:07:24.877 --> 00:07:26.812
Please assemble your tissues."

00:07:26.812 --> 00:07:30.449
Yeah, yeah, yeah. It's a very
astute point, actually.

00:07:30.449 --> 00:07:32.151
If it makes it to the planes.

00:07:32.151 --> 00:07:34.553
How do they choose
what goes on there?

00:07:34.553 --> 00:07:35.988
-I don't know.
Do you want to ask now?

00:07:35.988 --> 00:07:37.456
-That's not for now.
-No, no. Let's --

00:07:37.456 --> 00:07:38.690
But, you know,
if you want to --

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:40.559
If you have any favorite
airlines that you want to,

00:07:40.559 --> 00:07:42.294
you know, basically ask
right now to have it.

00:07:42.294 --> 00:07:43.929
-I do, but I couldn't --

00:07:43.929 --> 00:07:46.765
I couldn't cheapen myself
in that way.

00:07:46.765 --> 00:07:48.834
-Very good. That was a trap.
We laid out a trap for you.

00:07:48.834 --> 00:07:50.469
-Thank you. Thanking you!
-Yeah.

00:07:50.469 --> 00:07:52.605
Um, another thing
about a film like this.

00:07:52.605 --> 00:07:54.740
You know, it's a very --
it's a very small cast,

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:56.908
and that must be
a wonderful part

00:07:56.908 --> 00:07:59.845
because, you know, you mentioned
doing a play, you know,

00:07:59.845 --> 00:08:01.613
with Phoebe or working
with Paul on stage.

00:08:01.613 --> 00:08:05.484
It must at some point feel
a little bit like not a play,

00:08:05.484 --> 00:08:07.653
but a play's cast.
Like, a small group
of people that get to...

00:08:07.653 --> 00:08:09.221
-Yeah, well,
if there's only the four of you,

00:08:09.221 --> 00:08:10.655
you could get sick
looking at each other.

00:08:10.655 --> 00:08:12.091
And then -- [ Laughs ]

00:08:12.091 --> 00:08:15.494
But the subject matter
is so epic, you know?

00:08:15.494 --> 00:08:19.165
You'll see it when you guys
go and see the movie.

00:08:19.165 --> 00:08:21.734
Uh, you know,
it's such a huge subject matter.

00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:23.702
So it's kind of nice,
the juxtaposition between

00:08:23.702 --> 00:08:26.438
these tiny little things,
these stories that we think

00:08:26.438 --> 00:08:28.607
within our own families
are just so precious to us,

00:08:28.607 --> 00:08:30.276
but actually, you know,
they're huge.

00:08:30.276 --> 00:08:31.510
You know, our own life story

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:33.178
is the most important thing
in our world.

00:08:33.178 --> 00:08:35.514
So, um, yeah, it was
really, really intimate.

00:08:35.514 --> 00:08:37.249
And we shot it
in the childhood home --

00:08:37.249 --> 00:08:38.783
It's about this man
who goes back

00:08:38.783 --> 00:08:40.419
to his childhood home.

00:08:40.419 --> 00:08:42.721
And we shot it
in the childhood home,

00:08:42.721 --> 00:08:44.924
actual childhood home
of our director, Andrew Haigh.

00:08:44.924 --> 00:08:46.458
-No way.
-So we went into this little

00:08:46.458 --> 00:08:48.560
suburban house, and he was --

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:50.629
Of course, he wrote the film,

00:08:50.629 --> 00:08:53.999
and he was imagining his
own house, as you might do.

00:08:53.999 --> 00:08:56.636
And then he knocked on the door
of his childhood house,

00:08:56.636 --> 00:08:59.638
and he was like,
"Hello. I used to live here."

00:08:59.638 --> 00:09:01.207
And mercifully,
they hadn't put a big

00:09:01.207 --> 00:09:03.375
disgusting extension onto it.
-Yeah.

00:09:03.375 --> 00:09:06.445
-And so, uh, we were able
to use it as a 1980s home.

00:09:06.445 --> 00:09:07.713
-Really?
-So it was extraordinary!

00:09:07.713 --> 00:09:09.381
-I kept looking at him going,
"Are you not --

00:09:09.381 --> 00:09:11.984
Is this not freaking you out
that you would have, like,

00:09:11.984 --> 00:09:14.219
been crying
in this little bathroom

00:09:14.219 --> 00:09:16.489
and all our crew's
stomping all over it?"

00:09:16.489 --> 00:09:18.658
But actually it provided
a real intimacy

00:09:18.658 --> 00:09:20.092
to the atmosphere, you know,

00:09:20.092 --> 00:09:22.227
because you feel like you
were really --

00:09:22.227 --> 00:09:23.596
Even though
it's not a true story,

00:09:23.596 --> 00:09:25.230
you feel like you're
representing somebody.

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:26.766
-Well, it was really
something special.

00:09:26.766 --> 00:09:28.467
I'm so glad I saw it.
Everybody should see it.

00:09:28.467 --> 00:09:30.135
Thanks for being here, man.
What a pleasure.

00:09:30.135 --> 00:09:31.970
-Thank you so much.
-Andrew Scott, everyone.

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:34.072
"All of Us Strangers"
